A||||11||ezsurf||88 Coupe, is it worth it?||||5||
Z||000000||ezsurf||05-30-2000||10:56 PM||||I found an 88 4 cyl coupe with 66K miles on it.  It has a sunroof, but other than that it's pretty plain Jane.  No pwr windows, no pwr door locks, no pwr mirrors, and no wing (just the luggage rack).  Also, since it's a coupe, no aero package.  I've been thinking of picking up an 88 model and putting a Northstar in it.  I was hoping for something with a more aggressive look, like the aero package.  Personally, I've never seen the back of an 88 coupe.  I was wondering if the tail lights are the same as the 88 GT.  I don't know if it would be worth it.  Anyone have any thoughts or suggestions? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula 18||05-30-2000||11:50 PM||||I think it would be a fun Sleeper car. You know that little commuter car that is a Mustang Killer in disguise.<p>[This message has been edited by Formula 18 (edited 05-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula 18||05-30-2000||11:50 PM||||First off the Fastback GT and the coupe have diffrent rear ends. I am pretty sure that SE's and notch-back GT's look pretty similar to a 88 coupe's. Second, this might make a good car to do the Swap with because it is a Plain Jane you would hae to worry about "ruining" a collectors car like an Indy. Moreover, it has the new style suspension & brakes. And pretty low miles on the body so I would guess that everything should be in good condition.  <BR>Brian <P>P.S. Keep me up dated on how it goes. ||||reg||14
Z||000003||2M4 Dale||05-30-2000||11:57 PM||||Put a wing on it and you've got a FORMULA !||||reg||1
Z||000004||ezsurf||05-31-2000||12:22 AM||||I thought about the Formula trick.  A couple of decals and wing, and you have a instant Formula.  I'll give it some thought.  I sure would like pwr windows and door locks.  My 86 se doesn't have pwr windows or door locks either.  I know I could upgrade it with these mods, but something tells me this would not be a pleasant experience.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||05-31-2000||01:01 AM||||My first Fiero was an '88 Coupe...Still have it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Rear top is same as all notchbacks I believe. Slightly different lower panel(?)<P>How much are they asking? I payed WAY too much for mine...$3k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Hey, I knew nothing about Fiero's then...(Still don't! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) It had 130k on it with a rebuilt engine, side scoop, and wing. Lots of other stuff fixed too. <P>After learning a little about the DIS Tech 4 and putting lots of work into the car, It runs great, handles great and gets 36 mpg to boot! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  It drives better than my '87 GT; Not as strong but the 4 banger, when running right, is not too bad. 300lbs lighter too. <P>With only 66k on it, depending on asking price, I think it would be worth it.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||05-31-2000||09:20 AM||||The 88 Coupe is a lot of fun, and mine has been very reliable too.  Ride and handling are somewhat better than earlier years and the brakes are much better - but suspension and brake parts are also more expensive and harder to find.<P>I'd buy it if I were you - you'll need those vented rotors to stop a Northstar. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||ezsurf||05-31-2000||09:25 PM||||This car is not in my local area.  I won't get a chance to look at it until sometime next week (maybe) and then the car may not be available.  I was told the car was priced at $1600, but that is unconfirmed.  I'm trying to find out more info on the Northstar conversion, to see if it's going to be feasible.  I don't know of anyone who has done the conversion.  If anyone knows of someone who has, I would like to know to see if it was worth the trouble.  Design 1 is the only company I know of who is doing this mod.  Anyone know of someone who has a Northstar?||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||05-31-2000||10:47 PM||||I had the opportunity to meet Kevin of Design1 at our club's show 2 weeks ago. There were 3 of his Northstar conversions there. All in all, the workmanship was impressive and Kevin is a good guy, very easy to talk to. I would love to say that I had the opportunity to testdrive, but no such luck. The weird thing is, they don't sound as earthshaking as a good ol pushrod V8 (or even 3.8 V6). Actually rather mild sounding. But if you are lookingor top speed and cool factor then I guess it can't be beat.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ezsurf||05-31-2000||11:30 PM||||Actually, I was primarily interested in the Northstar because of the weight.  Power to weight ratio is what I'm looking for.  The Northstar is lighter than the iron 2.8 V-6. I'm afraid, with a 350 chevy, the car will handle a bit slugish.  Plus, there has to be more room under the hood for the Northstar.  The 350 really looks stuffed in the engine bay.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||06-01-2000||07:11 AM||||Well, another guy in our club has a carburated ZZ4 "crate" 350 in his fiero, looks like there is more space in it then the Northstars, and it turns low 13s at the track in very streetable trim.  Don't get me wrong, I'm not trying to badmouth Design1, especially since I yearn for his turbo kit on my 2.8. I just wouldn't call the pushrod V8 sluggish. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ezsurf||06-01-2000||06:25 PM||||I'm not saying a chevy 350 is sluggish running, I'm saying I believe the extra weight will affect the handling of the car when you put it on a twisty road.  The 350 chevy is a great engine.  I haven't ruled out using a 350 chevy or other v-8.  If the Northstar is too costly, I'll find another engine.||||reg||1
